[{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/935963004/LaBraM/issues/comments/2540474659","html_url":"https://github.com/935963004/LaBraM/issues/47#issuecomment-2540474659","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/935963004/LaBraM/issues/47","id":2540474659,"node_id":"IC_kwDOLF0fBs6XbJEj","user":{"login":"935963004","id":38138106,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM4MTM4MTA2","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/38138106?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/935963004","html_url":"https://github.com/935963004","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/935963004/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/935963004/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/935963004/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/935963004/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/935963004/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/935963004/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/935963004/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/935963004/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/935963004/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2024-12-13T03:44:26Z","updated_at":"2024-12-13T03:44:26Z","body":"1. This is an interesting question. The tokenizer can be regarded as a denoiser as EEG signals are noisy. We input noisy EEG signals, and then get clean codes. Those codes, although clean, may lose information such as correlations between different patches, as they are independent. Therefore, we use the codes to serve as the target when performing masked EEG modeling like BERT. We have not tried use the codes as input directly, but in my view, this way may not get satisfactory results.\r\n2. You are correct.\r\n3. I think so. If you want to train it with multiple modalities, maybe disentangling learning would be beneficial. It is okay to only use CNN, but in this work, we want the architecture to be consistent and it does work using transformer.\r\n4. It is possible, but reconstructing the amplitude and phase can be easier for the model the learn when your data is insufficient and your model is somewhat small. We did this ablation in Appendix. ","author_association":"OWNER","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/935963004/LaBraM/issues/comments/2540474659/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/935963004/LaBraM/issues/comments/2540485824","html_url":"https://github.com/935963004/LaBraM/issues/47#issuecomment-2540485824","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/935963004/LaBraM/issues/47","id":2540485824,"node_id":"IC_kwDOLF0fBs6XbLzA","user":{"login":"dyhan316","id":34816251,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM0ODE2MjUx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/34816251?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316","html_url":"https://github.com/dyhan316","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2024-12-13T03:49:44Z","updated_at":"2024-12-13T03:49:44Z","body":"Thank you so much for your response. I just have one more further question regarding 2. if you don't mind. \r\n\r\n2. To be precis